{"reference":"Ephesians 6","book":"Ephesians","chapter":6,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Household authority and its limits","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The instructions address children as moral hearers and require fathers not to provoke them, but to nurture them. They also speak within the reality of enslavement rather than abolishing it: enslavers are told to stop threatening and are reminded that they and those they enslave share one Master. These limits matter, but they do not make slavery humane or authorize ownership, exploitation, abuse, or absolute parental control.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 6:1-4","Ephesians 6:5","Ephesians 6:5-9","Ephesians 6:9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_006_4934d5c973cfeeaba9eff730"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Armor, powers, and prayer","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The armor imagery is built around truth, righteousness, peace, faith, salvation, God’s word, and sustained prayer. It is therefore not a mandate for religious violence. The passage’s “powers” and “spiritual hosts” remain debated, as does the precise background of its imagery; although Artemis institutions were prominent in Ephesus, the letter never names Artemis, so the imagery should not simply be reduced to that cult.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 6:10-20","Ephesians 6:12","Ephesians 6:20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_006_97c7e6d24731d70efaa0ce64"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The call to prayer includes a request for bold speech while the letter closes with peace, love, faith, and grace. Read together, these features distinguish truthful witness from coercion, harassment, forced conversion, nationalism, or partisan capture. Spiritual-warfare language likewise should not be used to demonize political opponents, people of other religions, mental illness, disability, or ordinary disagreement.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 6:10-20","Ephesians 6:18-20","Ephesians 6:23-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_006_5f1ab7043b5e49eaf29cdba9"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Reading unequal households carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Households could encompass kinship, children, property, labor, freed people, enslaved people, dependency, and cult, often in coercively unequal arrangements. That setting helps explain why the passage addresses several household relationships, while cautioning against turning those arrangements into timeless hierarchy or softening slavery into ordinary employment. Related accounts distinguish healing, exorcism, failed invocation of a powerful name, confession, and voluntary destruction of books; they do not warrant treating magic as an ethnic trait.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 6:1-9","Ephesians 6:10-20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_006_ff2bc59d6a4da0560b3d9e65"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"Tychicus’s role","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Tychicus is presented as a beloved brother and faithful minister who will report on Paul’s circumstances and encourage the readers. The notice identifies him as a trusted messenger, but it does not provide enough information to reconstruct a complete itinerary, delivery circuit, or publication history.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 6:21-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_006_b693a653e3c24e0098b2f932"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"A caution from the letter’s reception","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Later uses of Ephesians to support antisemitism, marital domination, child abuse, slavery, or violent spiritual warfare go beyond—and can contradict—the letter’s commands concerning peace, love, truth, and the refusal of threats.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 5:21-33"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_006_fa3d542dd66a4a7ac2c2f436"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}